          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I get a heavy squeezing feeling behind my breastbone when I walk uphill that eases when I stop. How urgent is that, and what does guidance describe about prevention afterwards if it turns out to be angina?",
          "answer": "**Please see a clinician soon.** Chest heaviness that comes on when you walk uphill and eases when you rest needs a medical assessment soon, not whenever it is convenient. The automated risk check gave a routine result, but that is not a clearance. The Coronary artery disease summary describes this pattern as classic stable angina, and the Physical activity for high blood pressure note advises that anyone with chest pain on exertion be assessed before starting or increasing vigorous activity. Until you have been seen, avoid pushing through the discomfort.\n\n**Call emergency services immediately** if the squeezing lasts more than a few minutes or goes away and comes back, if it happens at rest or with much less effort than usual, or if it spreads to your arm, shoulder, neck, jaw, back or upper abdomen. Also call if it comes with breathlessness, cold sweat, nausea, vomiting, light-headedness, a sense of impending doom, or if you feel faint. The Chest pain that may indicate a heart attack guidance lists these signs, and the condition reference adds that waiting to see whether the discomfort settles is a common and costly mistake. Symptoms can be less typical in women, older people and people with diabetes, so do not rely on them looking classic.\n\n**On prevention:** I could not retrieve guidance on managing angina specifically. The secondary prevention guidance I did find is written for people who already have confirmed coronary disease, such as a previous heart attack, a stent, bypass, or narrowed arteries on a scan. It describes a combined package: long-term antiplatelet therapy, high-intensity statins, cardiac rehabilitation, smoking cessation support, yearly influenza vaccination, diabetes management if present, and review at least yearly or sooner after any change in symptoms. Which symptom medicines come first, and any stent or bypass decisions, depend on your own test results and belong with your clinician. The passages I found give no dosing.\n\n**Where authorities differ:** US and European guidance describe cholesterol targets differently. US guidance uses statin intensity and percentage reduction, while European guidance sets explicit LDL goals and may add combination therapy. They also define high blood pressure differently: US guidance uses 130/80 mm Hg as the diagnostic threshold, while European guidance uses 140/90 mm Hg for office readings. I found no disagreement about antiplatelet therapy or angina medicines, and I could not find evidence-strength grades for these recommendations, so I cannot say how strong the evidence behind each one is.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
